1 NOTE {geni:about_me} =Sophie Axelsdatter Brahe=
2 CONT
2 CONT * Daughter of Axel Ottesen Brahe and Mette Falksdatter Gøye
2 CONT * Sophie Axelsdatter Brahe (born 11 May 1578 at Elvedgaard, died 21 December 1646 in Copenhagen ) was a Danish noblewoman. She was the daughter of the councilor Axel Ottesen Brahe of Elvedgaard and his wife Mette Falksdatter Gøye 
2 CONC . The astronomer Tycho Brahe was her paternal uncle, and her namesake Sophie Brahe was her aunt.
2 CONT
2 CONT =Married=
2 CONT
2 CONT * Sophie was '''married to Holger Rosenkrantz of Rosenholm near Randers on 13 June 1598.''' The wedding took place at Malmøhus , where her sisters Birgitte and Anne were simultaneously married to Henrik Gøye and Eiler Qvitzow, res
2 CONC pectively.
2 CONT
2 CONT ==[https://no.m.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sophie_Axelsdatter_Brahe Sophie Axelsdatter Brahe]
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT In connection with Wallenstein's occupation of Jutland in the years 1625-29, the Rosenholm family left and took up residence with relatives in Scania. They then returned, and Rosenkrantz resigned as councillor and gave up his post
2 CONC  as sheriff at Odensegård, which caused the family some financial worries.
2 CONT Like many other wives of Danish nobles in the 17th century, she was a mature and independent woman. Due to her husband's obligations as a councillor and his theological studies, Sophie became the one who managed the family life an
2 CONC d the estate. Rosenholm functioned as a kind of school for over 50 noble and bourgeois children, which under Rosenkrantz developed into a spiritual powerhouse. Sophie contributed, among other things, as a foster mother and a cook.
2 CONT
2 CONT She gave birth to no fewer than 13 children between 1600 and 1617, eight of whom reached adulthood. In addition, she had four nephews in care after Gøye's death in 1611, and six nieces after Birgitte's death in 1619.
2 CONT Manuscripts
2 CONT
2 CONT The manuscripts left behind by Sophie were later included in Anne Gøye's collections, which later formed the core of Karen Brahe's library, now in Roskilde . [ 2 ] The large family archive from Rosenholm burned down in 1826. Howev
2 CONC er, a large collection of transcripts of Rosenkrantz's scientific correspondence in particular has been preserved. Sophie's account book, which is now in the Royal Library , provides a good insight into the life of a Danish noble 
2 CONC family in the first half of the 17th century. The couple is buried in the family burial chapel in Hornslet Church in Jutland.
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT =Links=
2 CONT
2 CONT * http://da.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sophie_Axelsdatter_Brahe
