1 NOTE {geni:about_me} =Valdemar IV Atterdag Christoffersen, King of Denmark=
2 CONT
2 CONT * Son of Christopher II, King of Denmark and Euphemia of Pomerania, Queen Of Denmark
2 CONT * Valdemar was raised by the German Emperor Ludwig of Bavaria from 1326-38. In 1938 he to his brother-in-law, Margrave Ludwig of Brandenburg, a son of the Emperor. Valdemar IV was installed as king in 1340 by agreement with the Ho
2 CONC lstein mortgagees. With the help of tax collections, patient negotiation, armed intervention and the sale of Estonia for 19,000 marks of silver in 1346, he succeeded in reuniting Denmark
2 CONT * Denmark. Valdemar 4. Atterdag arms in Gelre's arms book 1370-86. A dannebrog plume is stuck through a fan of peacock feathers on one of the helmet's ermine-covered ship's horns. It is the oldest known colored depiction of the Da
2 CONC nish flag. Valdemar Atterdag was probably also the first Danish king to use Dannebrog. Because of his relationship with the emperor, he became aware of the red banner of the Holy Roman Empire with a white cross; it had developed s
2 CONC hortly before 1200 from the kingdom's originally all-red flag, in which the emperor put a white cross when he went on crusade.
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT ==[https://sok.riksarkivet.se/?Sokord=Svenske&EndastDigitaliserat=false&AvanceradSok=False&FacettFilter=register_facet%24Brev%3A&FacettState=xXo49g%3Ac%7Cundefined%3Ac%7C&page=139&postid=sdhk_7825&tab=post#tab '''SDHK no: 7825''']
2 CONT
2 CONT * '''Dating: 1360 August 10'''
2 CONT * '''Place of issue: In front of Helsingborg Castle''' 
2 CONT
2 CONT Content
2 CONT
2 CONT '''King Valdemar of Denmark announces that he and his heirs have entered into an alliance with Duke Albrekts of Mecklenburg and his heirs. In the alliance, on King Valdemar's side, his son Kristofer, Duke of Lolland, and "uncle" D
2 CONC uke Erik of Saxony(-Lauenburg) and on Duke Albrekt's side the sons Henrik, Albrekt and Magnus, Dukes of Mecklenburg, are admitted. Other named princes, including all the counts of Holstein, are also offered the opportunity to join
2 CONC . (...). It is decided that King Valdemar and Duke Kristofer shall assist Duke Albrekt and his sons in obtaining satisfactory compensation from the King and Kingdom of Sweden for alleged losses. The duke and his sons shall, for th
2 CONC eir part, help King Valdemar and his son Kristofer in their complaints against the Swedish king and the kingdom of Sweden. (...). See also DS no. 6333.'''
2 CONT
2 CONT '''The issuer and duke Kristofer and the pledges seal.'''
2 CONT
2 CONT * '''Source: Original:  Or. perg., Mecklenburgisches Landeshauptarchiv, Schwerin, Printed: ST II, ​​no. 349 , Mekl. UB no. 8775 , DD III:5, no. 352 , DS 6333. Reg. Dan. Looks. II, No. *2377. Literature and Commentary, see DS no. 6
2 CONC 333'''
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT ==[https://denstoredanske.lex.dk/Dannebrog Denmark. Valdemar 4. Atterdag Arms in Gelre's Arms Book 1370-86]
2 CONT
2 CONT In a Dutch book of arms (Gelre) that was created after 1370, a red banner with a white cross is seen as part of the helmet sign on Valdemar 4 's coat of arms . It is the oldest known colored reproduction of the Dannebrog. In anoth
2 CONC er Dutch coat of arms (Bellenville), where the oldest coats of arms seem to date back to the years between 1330 and 1340, the Danish royal coat of arms shows a shield with a cross and the word Dannebrog; the background color of th
2 CONC e shield is indicated by a letter as red. The oldest known Dannebrog must be linked to Valdemar 4. , who was king 1340-75.
2 CONT
2 CONT Since the middle of the 12th century, the German emperor had used an all-red banner. When he went on crusade , a white cross was put in it. From the end of the 13th century, the imperial red-white cross flag can be considered the 
2 CONC permanent German flag, which it still was in the 15th century. In the Middle Ages , you could meet a red weapon with a white cross in several places in the outskirts of the Holy Kingdom , and they relate in some way to the German 
2 CONC emperor. An example is the coat of arms of Savoy and corresponding banner , reflecting a close political relationship between the Emperor and the Count of Savoy. Before Valdemar 4.became king, he was attached to the emperor and hi
2 CONC s son, the Margrave of Brandenburg . They had a decisive influence on the negotiations that in 1340 led to Valdemar being recognized as king of Denmark. He had good reason to nurture his relationship with the imperial power. By ad
2 CONC opting the same red banner with a white cross that the emperor used, he could send a political signal to those to whom the Danish kingdom was pledged, and other German princes and the Hanseatic States .
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT '''The legend of Dannebrog'''
