2 NOTE 13) (Fig. 46) Niels Strangesen, til Norringtoft, af Slægten Bild, Lensmand paa Hillerslev og Ørum63. »Hic iacet d(omi)n(u)s Nicola(u)s Stra(n)ges(en) miles, qui obiit an(n)o D(omi)ni mcdxxiiii cu(m) uxore sua d(omin)a Ingeburg fil
3 CONC ia Folradi use(n)rades, q(ue) (obiit) an(n)o xl cu(m) utroru(m)q(ue) proge(n)ie, or(a)te p(ro) eis« (»Her ligger Ridder Niels Strangesen, som døde i Herrens Aar 1424, tilligemed sin Hustru Fru Ingeborg, Folrad Dusenrades Datter, s
3 CONC om døde [14]40, samt begges Børn, beder for dem«). Rød, ølandsk Kalksten, 223 × 123 cm, med reliefhuggede Minuskler i Rammebaand langs Stenens Kanter, brudt i Hjørnerne af Cirkler med Evangelisttegn, der holder Skriftbaand. I Midt
3 CONC feltet er kun Mandens Vaaben indristet, skraatstillet,
3 CONT hjelmet med spinkelt, baandagtigt Hjelmklæde. Stenen laa 1767 i søndre Korsfløj, »øverst op i Koret med et Hjørne ind under Dørstolpen til et Begravelse« (Abildgaard), nu er den opstillet ved søndre Sideskibs Østvæg.
3 CONT
3 CONT 13) (Fig. 46) Niels Strangesen, to Norringtoft, of klahn Bild, duke of Hillerslev and Ørum (63). »Hic iacet d(omi)n(u)s Nicola(u)s Stra(n)ges(en) miles, qui obiit an(n)o D(omi)ni mcdxxiiii cu(m) uxore sua d(omin) a Ingeburg filia 
3 CONC Folradi use(n)rades, q(ue) (obiit) an(n)o xl cu(m) utroru(m)q(ue) proge(n)ie, or(a)te p(ro) eis' ("Here lies Knight Niels Strangesen, who died in the Lord's year 1424, together with his wife Mrs. Ingeborg, daughter of Folrad Dusen
3 CONC rades, who died [14]40, as well as the children of both, pray for them"). Red, island limestone, 223 × 123 cm, with relief-carved minuscules in a frame band along the edges of the stone, broken in the corners by circles with Evang
3 CONC elist signs holding a script band. In the middle field, only the male's coat of arms is engraved, slanted, the helmet with flimsy, band-like helmet-cloth.
3 CONT The stone was laid in 1767 in the south transept, "at the top of the chancel with a corner under the doorpost for a burial" (Abildgaard), now it is placed by the east wall of the south aisle.
