1 OCCU lensmand på Københavns Slot fra 1514
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Torben Oxe (død 29. november 1517) var søn af Johan Oxe og Inger Bille.
2 CONT
2 CONT Han var Christian 2.'s lensmand på Københavns Slot fra 1514 og fik desuden Krogen (Kronborg) len ved Helsingør. Fængslet af kongen, der for rigsrådet anklagede ham for bl.a. giftmord på Dyveke, men han blev frifundet for anklagen.
2 CONT
2 CONT Han blev af kongen bragt for en birketingsret af 12 bønder fra landsbyen Solbjerg og dømt: "Vi dømmer ham ikke, men hans gerninger dømmer ham".
2 CONT
2 CONT Torben Oxe blev halshugget den 29. november 1517 i nærheden af nutidens Hauser Plads. Rigsrådet misbilligede henrettelsen.
2 CONT
2 CONT https://da.wikipedia.org/wiki/Torben_Oxe 
2 CONT
2 CONT http://denstoredanske.dk/Dansk_Biografisk_Leksikon/Samfund,_jura_og_politik/Myndigheder_og_politisk_styre/Lensmand/Torben_Oxe 
2 CONT
2 CONT https://finnholbek.dk/getperson.php?personID=I52375&tree=2 
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT * Carlquist, Gunnar, red (1937). Svensk uppslagsbok. Bd 20. Malmö: Svensk Uppslagsbok AB. sid. 1067
2 CONT
2 CONT Torben Oxe, död 11 november 1517, var en dansk adelsman. Han var farbror till Peder Oxe. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Torben Oxe gjorde sig känd som en våldsam godsrövare. 1511 tvingade han till exempel Axel Tott att avstå gods, vilket dock upphävdes genom rättartingsdom. Torben Oxe tillhörde Kristian II:s hovfolk och blev länsman i Köpenhamns sl
2 CONC ott och län. År 1517 anklagades han av kungen och blev efter frikännande av riksrådet dömd till döden av en bondedomstol. Hans brott var enligt en senare utbildad tradition, att han stått efter Kristian II:s älskarinna Dyveke Sigb
2 CONC ritsdatter gunst eller att han förgiftat henne. Enligt nyare forskning fälldes dödsdomen över Torben Oxe på grund av ej närmare kända ekonomiska transaktioner, där han skulle ha visat oärlighet; om domen fällt med rätta, kan ej av
2 CONC göras. 
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT Torben Oxe (died 29 November 1517) was a Danish nobleman and a member of an aristocratic family.[1]
2 CONT
2 CONT Oxe was the son of Johan Oxe of Tordsø and Inger Torbensdatter Bille. Both his father and grandfather had served as Danish Councillors (dansk rigsråd). His nephew Peder Oxe, would become the future Danish finance minister and Stew
2 CONC ard of the Realm. Torben Oxe served as a liege man of King Christian II of Denmark, under whom from 1514, he was governor of Copenhagen Castle. He also inherited a fief in Kronborg from his father who died in 1490. [2]
2 CONT
2 CONT During the summer of 1517, Torben Oxe was accused of murdering Dyveke Sigbritsdatter, King Christian II's mistress, with some poisoned cherries. Dyveke's mother Sigbrit Willoms, the widow of a Dutch merchant, acted as an advisor t
2 CONC o the king, to the displeasure of much of the Danish nobility.[3][4]
2 CONT
2 CONT Torben Oxe was first tried and acquitted by the State Council. King Christian II did not accept the judgment and had Oxe indicted by a court consisting of a jury from Solbjerg outside Copenhagen. Oxe was charged in what amounted t
2 CONC o a justice-of-the-peace court with vague offences against his liege lord, Christian II. The verdict as directed by the king was guilty and the death sentence imposed. He was condemned to death, traditionally in the words: "Vi døm
2 CONC mer ham ikke, men hans gerninger dømmer ham" (We do not condemn him - his deeds condemn him) beheaded and buried in the graveyard of St. Gertrude's Hospital (St Gertruds Kloster) in Copenhagen. [5]
2 CONT
2 CONT Members of the Royal Council of the State (Rigsraadet) disapproved of the execution of Oxe, who was a popular figure. The execution further alienated Christian II from the nobles and the people of Copenhagen. Thereafter the king l
2 CONC ost no opportunity to suppress the nobility and raise commoners to power.
2 CONT
2 CONT A famous painting by Eilif Peterssen titled Christian II Signing the Death Warrant of Torben Oxe is present in the National Museum of Art, Architecture and Design in Oslo, Norway.
2 CONT
2 CONT References	
2 CONT
2 CONT # ^ Torben Oxe (Biographies and Denmark's history from the Oldenburg kings )
2 CONT # ^ Torben Oxe (Den Store Danske)
2 CONT # ^ Dyveke (ca. 1490 - 1517) Dyveke Villomsdatter (Dansk Kvindebiografisk Leksikon)
2 CONT # ^ Villoms, Sigbrit ( - ca. 1532) (Dansk Kvindebiografisk Leksikon)
2 CONT # ^ S. Gertruds Hospital og Kapel (Kjøbenhavns Kirker og Klostre i Middelalderen) 
2 CONT
2 CONT * Lockhart, Paul Douglas Denmark, 1513-1660: the rise and decline of a Renaissance monarchy (Oxford University Press, 2007) ISBN 0-19-927121-6 
2 CONT * Bain, Robert Nisbet Scandinavia: a political history of Denmark, Norway and Sweden from 1513 to 1900 (Cambridge: University Press, 1905)  
2 CONT
2 CONT * Christian II Signing the Death Warrant of Torben Oxe painting by Eilif Peterssen, 1875-76 
2 CONT
