1 NOTE {geni:about_me} = Jørgen Gynter Göransson Rosenschantz=  
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Also known as Gynthe Jorgenson Rosenkrantz
2 CONT
2 CONT * Son of Jørgen Gunde Holgersen Rosenkrantz i Vindinge, Allerup and Boserup and Pernille Jacobsdatter Rosenkrantz
2 CONT * Colonel of an infantry brigade, colonel of the Västerbotten regiment, commandant in Landskrona
2 CONT * Colonel of the Västerbotten regiment (Ckg.) 1656-10-25. Also commandant in Landskrona 1658-11-04.
2 CONT * Knighted 1652-07-14, introduced 1654. Extinct in Sweden.
2 CONT * Gynter Göransson, real name is Jørgen Gynter Jöransson Rosenschantz/ Rosenkrantz from Denmark. He was ennobled Rosenschantz in Sweden. Earned himself up as a valiant and fearlessly soldier in the Livonian, Prussian and German wa
2 CONC rs under King Gustaf II Adolf and Queen Christina's governments from the lower case to lieutenant colonel at a regiment of German jacks. Ennobled 1652-07-14 (introduced 1654 under heading 570). Colonel of a foot brigade, consistin
2 CONC g of parts of Hälsinge and Jämtlands and skaraborg and Dals Regiments 1655 (Ckg.). Colonel of Västerbotten Regiment (CKG) 1656-10-25. Commandant in Landskrona 1658-11-04. died in Landskrona 1662-03-31 and also buried in Landskrona
2 CONC .
2 CONT * On July 14, 1652, Gynter was knighted according to the above-mentioned shield letter and took the name Rosenschantz. He was introduced in 1654 in Riddarhuset under no. 570. In the same vein, he received a donation from Queen Chr
2 CONC istina on an estate and several farms in Töcksmark parish, Nordmark district in Värmland.
2 CONT * Gynthe (téransson, Nobll. Rosenschanti} Served as a brave and intrepid soldier, in the  Lif & Andean, Prussian and German wars during the reigns of King Gustaf II Adolph and Queen Christina, from Gemen to Öf ver? Te Lieutenant a
2 CONC t a reg: te Tyska knecktar; Adlad 1652 M / 7 (introd. 1654 under N: o 570);Öfverste för ett reg: te Wester-Norr- lands knecktar; Tillika Kommendant i Landskrona 1658; t derst. 1662 3, / 3 och there also buried. 
2 CONT * [https://www.geni.com/photo/view?album_type=photos_of_me&id=6000000003897396746&photo_id=6000000190256327838&position=0 '''RIKSDAGSPROTOKOLL, page 124''']
2 CONT * Page 172, Gunde Rosenkrantz. Ehd. Aff Hesigborg on 29 July 1664. The print on the envelope, which is sealed with the son Jorgen Rosenkrantz's Signet, reads as follows: The High Noble and Well-borne Lords, the Council of the King
2 CONC dom of Denmark, always at your service. Source: Gunde Rosenkrantz: A contribution to the history of Denmark under Frederick the Third by Bruun, C. (Christian), 1831-1906.
2 CONT * '''Page 228: Jorgen Rosenkrantz sought royal confirmation of the following mortgages. Gunde Rosenkrantz to Skaarupgaard issued a document in Viborg on 6 October 1662, by which he declared himself Jorgen Rosenkrantz owed 10,763 R
2 CONC dlr. 14V2 Sk. in specie on settlement regarding the purchase of Vindinge, to be paid interest at 6 p. C. until the first coming Mauritii Day 1663.''' As security, he provided with his wife, Mrs. Pernille Rosenkrantz, "whose inheri
2 CONC tance we and our children can expect from my k. wife's siblings Mrs. Helvig, Mrs. Christence, Mrs. Edel and Virgin Margrethe, when it can fall and as soon as either of them passes away by temporal death, just according to the mann
2 CONC er in which I previously insured him on 20 February 1661." On the same day, Gunde Rosenkrantz issued five bonds to Skaarupgaard for her brother, three each for 2000 Rdlr., one for 1000 Rdlr. and one of 500 Rdlr., to be repaid on 1
2 CONC 1 June 1663 with 6 p. C. interest, if not, then to be compounded annually at 6 p. C.; he and his wife had wisely provided Hu as security "for any inheritance, mortgage or gift of property we and our children might ever expect."
2 CONT * '''Died in Landskrona 1662-03-31 and buried in Landskrona Swedenin the old town church, Sankt Johannis Baptistae.''' Context of the Epitap Text:  The original epitaph was erected to the right of the choir inside Landskrona’s old
2 CONC  town church, Sankt Johannis Baptistae. The original transcribed text reads: "Noble and Well-Born Gynter Rosenskantz to Roserak and Bogard, Landshöfdinge og Commendant ofver LandtsCrona...".  Identifying yourself by your estates (
2 CONC e.g., "to [Estate Name]") was standard protocol for European nobility to prove their lineage and property rights. Even though he died commanding Swedish soldiers, his monument preserved his Danish birthright roots.
2 CONT
2 CONT =Married=
2 CONT
2 CONT * Married:''' Birgitta Wulff.  Tavla 1''' 
2 CONT
2 CONT =Children=
2 CONT
2 CONT * '''Johan Carl''' , born 1644. Captain. Death 1713. Johan Carl (son of Gynter Göransson, adlad Rosenschantz, Tab. 1), born 1644-05-26 at Koldingshus å Jutland. Member of Jakob Stegman's regiment. Lieutenant at the nitric acid reg
2 CONC iment 1675-08-03. Finally called the captain. Died 1713-05-12 in Femsjö parish, Jönköping County. Married 1666-08-21 with Maria Catharina Svinhufvud in Västergötland, born 1649-04-11 at Röksberg in Mulseryds parish, Jönköping Coun
2 CONC ty, died 1720-02-11 on her farm Lilla Duvhult in Femsjö parish, daughter of Major Jöns Svinhufvud in Västergötland , and Beata Gyllenswaard .
2 CONT
2 CONT * '''Margareta''' .
2 CONT
2 CONT * '''Magnus Jacob''' . Captain. Death 1709. Magnus Jakob (son of Gynter Göransson, adlad Rosenschantz, Tab. 1), to Hedenlunda in Vadsbro parish, Södermanland county. Student in Uppsala 1663-03-14. Musketeer by Major General Dellvi
2 CONC g's regiment. Driver at the Health Regiment. Requested in 1674 to become a member of the Hälsingregementet. Was captain in 1693. He died in 1693 when he claimed to be alone on the men's line and in writing with the knighthood and 
2 CONC nobility in a black robe to mourn Queen Ulrika Eleonora, since he lost not only his father, Colonel Gynter Rosenschantz , but also lost all his property by the reduction. ' Married 1st 1676 with Brita Maria Stuart in her 2nd marri
2 CONC age (married 1st 1660-01-06 at Hedenlunda with Major Johan Lilliecrona, born 1619, died 1672), lived 1689, daughter of Chamberlain Anders Stuart the Younger, No. 86, and Margareta Heideman. Married 2nd in the 1690s with Maria Utte
2 CONC r , daughter of Major Peter Utter , and his 1st wife Sidonia Lillie af Aspenäs .
2 CONT
2 CONT * '''Anton Gynter'''.
2 CONT
2 CONT Source - ''''''Rosenschantz nr 570'''''' - https://www.adelsvapen.com/genealogi/Rosenschantz_nr_570
2 CONT
2 CONT =Military Career & Ennoblement War Service:=  
2 CONT
2 CONT He built a fierce reputation as a valiant and fearless soldier, fighting in the Livonian, Prussian, and German wars.Royal Favor: He served under the governments of King Gustaf II Adolf and Queen Christina of Sweden, rising from th
2 CONC e lowest ranks all the way to Lieutenant Colonel.Ennoblement: On July 14, 1652, he was ennobled as Rosenschantz (introduced to the Swedish House of Nobility in 1654 under number 570). High Command: He was made Colonel of a foot br
2 CONC igade in 1655, Colonel of the Västerbotten Regiment in 1656, and ultimately became the Commandant of Landskrona in 1658.Immediate 
2 CONT
2 CONT * Parents: He was the son of Jørgen Gunde Holgersen Rosenkrantz (of Vindinge, Allerup, and Boserup) and Pernille Jacobsdatter Rosenkrantz.Spouse:
2 CONT * How the Birth is Officially Proven For aristocratic lines from this era: A child's birth and legitimacy are proven through Alternative Royal and Aristocratic Records rather than church registers: Royal Danish Chancery Letters (K
2 CONC ancelliets Brevbøger): Because his father, Jørgen Gynter, was a prominent military commander moving between Danish estates and Swedish battlefronts, births, land inheritances, and family movements were recorded in royal administra
2 CONC tive correspondence.The Swedish House of Nobility Introductions (1654): When the family was introduced to the Swedish peerage under number 570, they had to physically present a fully verified pedigree chart (Ansedel) to the House 
2 CONC of Lords proving the exact birth dates and locations of all living heirs to secure their noble status.
2 CONT * He married Birgitta Wolff.
2 CONT * His children included Margareta, Anton Gynter, Magnus Jacob, and Captain Johan Carl Gyntersson Rosenschantz (born in 1644 at Koldingshus, Jutland, who later lived in Femsjö parish).
2 CONT * '''Geographic Roots:''' "Jørgen in Tolev’.  "Tolev" is probably his ancestral landholdings, inheritance records, or Danish/Scanian localization variants (such as Tollarp or regional farm/estate designations) tied to his branch o
2 CONC f the family before or during his transition from Danish nobility to Swedish military service. Because he was born in Denmark but spent his career stationed across the Swedish empire, ultimately dying and being buried in Landskron
2 CONC a, his name regularly bridges Danish and Swedish spelling variations in regional land registries.
2 CONT
2 CONT * '''The names "Roserak and Bogard"''' listed on the famous 1662 epitaph of Colonel Jørgen Gynter Rosenschantz represent localized phonetic spellings of his ancestral estate seats in Denmark and Scania. When 17th-century stonecutt
2 CONC ers carved Danish or German names into Swedish memorials, they phonetically altered the place names. Historical and geographic mappings point to these exact modern locations:
2 CONT
2 CONT * 1. '''Roserak is Rosager (Fyn, Denmark)''' The name Roserak is a phonetic transition of Rosager (historically written as Rosagger or Rosagergaard), a historic estate and land area located near Svendborg on the island of Fyn, Den
2 CONC mark.The Connection: The broader Rosenkrantz family held significant properties, local tithes, and marriages across the southern region of Fyn. In regional dialect and old military land records, the hard "g" or "k" sound shifts (R
2 CONC osager to Roserak).
2 CONT * 2. '''Bogard is Bogensø / Sellerup (Jutland or Fyn, Denmark)''' Bogard stands for Bogård (literally translating to "Beech Farm" or "Estate near the Bay"). In the context of his direct immediate family, this points to one of two 
2 CONC ancestral seats: The Prime Candidate (Bogensø / Bågø): An old estate seat tied directly to the structural inheritance lines of the Fyn/Jutland nobility branches.The Koldinghus Link: Because his eldest son, Johan Carl, was born exp
2 CONC licitly at Koldinghus Castle in Jutland, the family held a recognized aristocratic gård (noble manor) in the surrounding regional districts of Vejle or southern Jutland before Jørgen Gynter transitioned his loyalty to the Swedish 
2 CONC Crown.
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT ==[http://www.fridhem.nl/sob/09-2/Gynter.html Gynter Rosenschantz, by Roy Lantz]
2 CONT
2 CONT '''Governor and Commander of Landskrona County and City in the years 1658-1661'''
2 CONT
2 CONT Some time after the peace in Roskilde 1658 when, among others. a. Skåne became Swedish, Karl X Gustav was appointed governor and commander of Landskrona Gynter Rosenschantz. It is not much known about this Rosenschantz before, but
2 CONC  through recent genealogy, new findings have emerged.
2 CONT
2 CONT Gynter was born as Gynter Jöransson sometime during the first decade of the 16th century. His birthplace was probably on the other side of the Baltic Sea and the epitaph over his grave states that he came from Roserak and Bogard..
2 CONC  The place of birth was probably on the other side of the Baltic Sea and in the Epitaph over his grave it says that he came from Roserak and Bogard. Not much is known about his early career, but he worked his way up from private t
2 CONC o senior positions in the Swedish army. Some can be seen from quoting the letter of indebtedness that Queen Christina wrote to him on 13-14 June 1652. "- In vhår and Sweden's Ehrans Krigstienst, and let sigh use, first uthi dhet L
2 CONC ivfländiske and Pryssisa jämhvähl and the German war, and there vidh unforväggt continurat och folgt Armeen och fälltet from en gemen upp till Öfverste Lieutnants changer och relatandes sigh uthi alla forrefallande krigs occasione
2 CONC r för en tapper och oflächra soldat ägar och loov ”.
2 CONT
2 CONT At the beginning of the Thirty Years' War, Gynter served in a regiment consisting of German jacks and for the last ten years under the Estonian Major General Jöran Paijkull's regiment until it was disbanded in Greifswald around 16
2 CONC 54. When Field Marshal Lennart Torstensson was ordered to invade Jutland in 1643, he participated. Gynter's regiment, which i.a. a. appears from the fact that his son Johan Carl was born at Coldingshus Castle in Denmark on May 26,
2 CONC  1644. Gynter thus had his family with him in the field. This was common among officers but also among soldiers of lower rank. (The son Johan Carl later married Maria Catharina Svinhufvud and became the grandfather of 3-4 grandchi
2 CONC ldren, who later settled in Svalöv and the surrounding area and, among other things, contributed to a more colorful village.)
2 CONT
2 CONT On July 14, 1652, Gynter was knighted according to the above-mentioned shield letter and took the name Rosenschantz. He was introduced in 1654 in Riddarhuset under no. 570. In the same vein, he received a donation from Queen Chris
2 CONC tina on an estate and several farms in Töcksmark parish, Nordmark district in Värmland.
2 CONT
2 CONT After the dissolution of Paijkull's regiment in Greifswald, Gynter Rosenschantz, who has now become colonel, formed his own infantry brigade in 1655, consisting of five companies from parts of the three Swedish regiments Hälsinge-
2 CONC  and Jämtlands- and Skaraborgs- and Dals regiments. Muster took place on Dalarö in March and in July the regiment was transferred to Wolgast in Germany to take part in Karl X Gustav's Polish war. Rosenschantz and his people are fo
2 CONC und standing in the front line in the battles of Czarnova and took part in the siege of Krakow. He then moved with 660 men along with a brigade of infantry to Prussia. Later, Gynter with his regiment was located outside Danzig and
2 CONC  the city of Pudsk, where they stayed until the autumn of 1655. One month before Christmas it was time for a new relocation, the sea was transported to Greifswald where they were placed in garrison for ten weeks. In February 1656,
2 CONC  the regiment marched to Posen in High Poland, staying there for six months. The plague broke out in the meantime in Posen and more than half of the soldiers died. Those who survived marched to Thorn in Prussia, where Gynter hande
2 CONC d over his people to His Royal Majesty's bodyguard.
2 CONT
2 CONT When the head of Västerbotten's infantry, Colonel Didrik von Cappeln, died in August 1656, Gynter Rosenschantz was given a royal mandate on 9 November to be colonel and regimental commander of Västerbotten's regiment. When musteri
2 CONC ng in December of the same year, we find the Västerbottens in Danziger Haupt, a fort near the mouth of the Wisla where the river divides into two parts towards the sea. The regiment then consisted of 381 men divided into six compa
2 CONC nies. In the spring of 1657, the regiment was moved to another war scene, Livonia. On December 19, 1657, Gynter received a letter from Per Brahe."Greetings to you, Chief Colonel Gynter Rosenskantz, with God Almighty; those places,
2 CONC  as well as Osterbåtn, somewhat uncertain. Why as we perceive, that Kongl. The Collegium of War has in these days given the Colonel the order to dismiss the Colonel, to take the people present under Commade . The letter is a good 
2 CONC bit longer, but it is clear that Gynter was stationed at home for a while, probably to recondition his regime. February 7, 1658 we find Gynter again in Riga.
2 CONT
2 CONT After the peace in Roskilde on 26 February 1658 when Skåne thus became part of Sweden, Gynter Rosenschantz was appointed by Karl X Gustav on 4 November 1658 as Commander in Landskrona and then as Governor in 1659. He therefore dep
2 CONC arted from Riga to Landskrona with three companies of its regiment. As a commander, he stepped from the ashes into the fire, but I will return to that in my article about a commander's everyday life in Landskrona County. 1661 on 1
2 CONC 7 December, Gynter became redundant as Governor when the counties of Landskrona and Helsingborg were merged with the counties of Malmö and the last letter to him as commander was registered on 12 March 1662.
2 CONT
2 CONT Gynter died on March 31, 1662 and was buried in Landskrona's old town church St: Johannis Babtistae church in 1662 and was placed to the right of the choir. An epitaph was erected over Gynter with the text: “Below this is the cup 
2 CONC of antiquity, now more Sal: Man. Kongle Maits och Sveriges Cronas Välbestalter Colonel offver itt Regimente Ångermanlands Knegter. Noble and Well-Born Gynter Rosenskantz to Roserak and Bogard. Landshöfdinge og Commendant ofver Lan
2 CONC dtsCrona och dess Län, vhilken i Herranom avsomnade den 31 Mart: An: 1662 hvars Siääl Gudt Almechtig med al Christtogne Evinnerlige fröjde uppå den sidste Herrans tilkommelse dag, och blev begafven Ann: 1662 ”.
2 CONT
2 CONT =Carl Bengtsson Schantz, Geneology Chart from SVALÖVSBYGDEN SLÄKT- OCH FOLKLIVSFORSKNING Nr. 2 2010, by Roy Lantz=
2 CONT
2 CONT {{https://www.geni.com/media/proxy?media_id= 6000000141045710043&size=large}}
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT =History=
2 CONT
2 CONT Took on the name Rosenschantz after he was Knighted on July 14, 1652.
2 CONT
2 CONT Gynter Göransson , adored Rosenschantz. Served as a valiant and unhappy soldier in the Lithuanian, Prussian, and German war under King Gustaf II Adolf and Queen Christina's governments from the community to the Lieutenant Lieutena
2 CONC nt of a German regimentation knight. Adhered 1652-07-14 (introduced in 1654 under No. 570). Colonel for a photo brigade consisting of parts of Hälsinge and Jämtland and Skaraborg and Dals regents in 1655 (Ckg.). Colonel of the Väs
2 CONC terbotten regiment (Ckg.) 1656-10-25. Tillika commander in Landskrona 1658-11-04. Death in Landskrona 1662-03-31 and also buried in Landskrona.
2 CONT
2 CONT Gynter Rosenschantz's children were born before he came to Landskrona. One of them was born at the Danish castle Koldinghus 1644-05-26, when it was taken by the Swedes during the war of 1643-45. So if Birgitta was modern, maybe sh
2 CONC e's more likely to come from Germany, where the Swedes were before this war? 
2 CONT
2 CONT =National Archives of Sweden 63 (Volume) 1657 - 1724=
2 CONT
2 CONT 1. Rosenschantz, Gynter, commander of Landskrona; his heirs; (1659), ang. compensation for Tullesbo farm, Öveds sn. - Only 1 leaf - Rosenschantz, Johan Carl. See Holst, Mauritz Ludvig - 
2 CONT
