1 NOTE {geni:about_me} =Holger Gundesson Rosenkrantz=
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT * Son of Gunde Holgersen Rosenkrantz, i Vindinge, Allerup and Boserup, Rigsraad and Pernille Jacobsdatter Rosenkrantz
2 CONT * The fourth eldest son, Holger, is mortally wounded by his father's side during the storm at Kjobenhavn. During the siege of Copenhagen he was present in the capital; various duties were assigned to him, and on the stormy night o
2 CONC f 11 February 1659 he bravely marched on the ramparts; he was saddened by the loss of his son Holger on this occasion.
2 CONT
2 CONT ==[https://runeberg.org/dbl/14/0219.html Rosenkrantz, Gunde, 1604-75, to Vindinge, Allerup and Boserup, Rigsraad, by Runeburg]
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT Rosenkrantz, Gunde, 1604—75, of Vindinge, Allerup and Boserup, Councillor, son of the aforementioned Councillor Holger R. (d. 1642), was born at Rosenholm on 2 Dec. 1604. In 1616 he travelled abroad for the first time and studied 
2 CONC at the Universities of Giessen and Strasbourg, as well as at the astronomical observatory in Lich; he returned home in 1619, but went abroad again the following year, first to Helmstedt, later to Leiden, where he matriculated in 1
2 CONC 621 and lived with the historian Meursius, but according to his own statement from 2 years later neglected his studies because of bad company. In 1624-25 he was at home in Denmark, but then in 1627-28 he accompanied the Councillor
2 CONC  Christian Thomesen Sehested and Jørgen Brahe on their mission to France and England. In April 1628 he became Secretary in the Danish Chancellery and then from March 1630 to December 1631 chamberlain to Duke Ulrik, whom he accompa
2 CONC nied on his journey to Germany. In 1632 he settled by marriage with his relative Pernille Rosenkrantz, daughter of Jacob R. of Arreskov and Pernille Gyldenstjerne, and in 1636 became county judge in Skaane and as such was granted 
2 CONC Heinekirke and Froste Herred. He also received a canonry at Lunde Cathedral Chapter, was often authorized for the Skåne nobility at estate meetings and was elected in 1638 as Skåne land commissioner, in which position he received 
2 CONC confirmation despite some displeasure in 1646. From 1648 he became more closely linked to Jutland, as he gave up his positions east of the Sound and received Kalø Fein; in 1650 he was authorized for the Jutland nobility. '''As ear
2 CONC ly as 1648 he had also received numerous votes from this when it recommended him for members of the Royal Council, and in June 1653 he was admitted to the council. When the war approached in 1657, he recruited a regiment of infant
2 CONC ry under his son Jørgen as colonel. When Carl Gustav occupied Odense in January 1658 he was there and was taken prisoner; he is said to have delivered "a beautiful and moving oration" that brought tears to the eyes of both Danes a
2 CONC nd Swedes, and the Swedish king responded by giving him back his card. During the siege of Copenhagen he was present in the capital; various duties were assigned to him, and on the stormy night of 11 February 1659 he bravely march
2 CONC ed on the ramparts; he was saddened by the loss of his son Holger on this occasion.'''
2 CONT
2 CONT He had amassed a considerable estate. He had inherited Skafføgaard in Jutland from his father; Vindinge (now Fuirendal) and Valbygaard (now Juellinge) in Zealand he had bought in 1645 and 1653 respectively; in Skaane he owned Alle
2 CONC rup and Boserup. Various traits give the impression that he was a rather heavy-handed man who did not look carefully at his means. A change of heart with the Lunde Cathedral Chapter does not show him from the brightest side, and b
2 CONC oth in Froste and Kalø Len he seems to have acted with great severity towards the farmers. He was a supplier to Holmen in Ulfeldt's time. But alongside all this GR had been deeply occupied with studies in theological, classical an
2 CONC d historical literature and with an extensive writing of his own. Just as he had taken a lively interest in his father's theological work, so too were some of his writings of religious content, though not dogmatic, but biblically 
2 CONC explanatory and edifying. The tone here is found in a number of other books; in them too, learning was combined with moral and religious exhortations, but their subjects were patriotic and political. The war  1643-45 thus gave him
2 CONC  occasion for 4 writings, one an appeal to abroad about Sweden's disloyalty, the second a praise of Christian IV after his victories at sea in 1644, the third and fourth a glorification of the peace of 1645. The mood that had rest
2 CONC ed on the last pieces had, however, disappeared again 12 years later. In the winter of 1657 GR was one of the most eager to incite to the frivolous war with Sweden; he anonymously sent out at the same time as the decisive Estates'
2 CONC  meeting in Odense in February. «A Patriot's Faithful Recollection to the Faithful Inhabitants of His Fatherland; in which he took the floor for the declaration of war and the necessity of making sacrifices. Soon, however, the mis
2 CONC fortunes came; his "beautiful and moving oration" in Odense barely a year later brought no relief; he saw the misery around him and in May 1658 wrote a letter from Middelfart to the Royal Council, in which he appealed to it to pro
2 CONC mote negotiations with Sweden in order to save the country. The new war, however, called him forth again to incite against the enemy; in 1659 he published in Latin and German a larger work on the law of Denmark and the treachery o
2 CONC f Sweden in addition to a smaller one, and in 1660 he wrote,at the government's own command, a completely lengthy work of similar content. All these contributions breathed of warm patriotism and an ingrained hatred of the enemy, b
2 CONC ut were marred by long, tiring quotations and a declamatory tone.
2 CONT
2 CONT However, it was not only the relationship with the foreign country that had brought GR to use his pen in these years of misfortune. With his inherited penchant for writing, with his considerable knowledge of state affairs abroad a
2 CONC nd at home, with his full sense of the prevailing political, military and economic emergency, he had written, although not published, essays that described the shortcomings and pointed out the desirable reforms. One of these essay
2 CONC s dates from the end of 1658 and the beginning of 1659. What it attacks most essentially is the harmful influence that has fallen to young and foreign advisors to the king; this threatens the constitution of the country, abuses ar
2 CONC e committed against the nobility and peasants, and the administration is dissolved; in addition, there is too much waste at court, and the army is in the worst order. As a means of getting out of this, he proposes savings, reorgan
2 CONC ization of the consulship and of the discharge, enlisted troops for garrison service and for wartime, but especially the establishment of government colleges according to Swedish pattern, but with preservation of the authority of 
2 CONC the Council of State. The second report is from the spring of 1660. It is somewhat less sharp in its criticism, it does not attack foreigners so much as it demonstrates the necessity of educating the nobility to skill in using the
2 CONC m; in return it takes a more positive view of important questions: the establishment of a standing enlisted army, the improvement of the crown's revenues, the payment of the state debt and the training of a higher civil service. S
2 CONC ome of the considerations here are indeed rather theoretical, but several of the proposals with regard to the fief and tax system and especially concerning relief for the peasantry by restrictions on the court and the abolition of
2 CONC  the levy on the crown estates testified to clear insight. On the whole, these contributions are a testimony to the best sides of GR; they are supported by a boldness and a political initiative, which was so rare among his peers, 
2 CONC and it does not diminish his honor that the Reichsrat did not dare to make his ideas their own.
2 CONT
2 CONT But during all this GR had become a financially ruined man. It seems as if he had speculated with his property purchases without having sufficient funds of his own. Even before the war his financial situation was in disarray; he c
2 CONC ould not pay his interest, and he leased out Vindinge in 1657. Things got worse with destruction of his estates and fiefs in the following years, and the exchange of Kalø fief in May 1660 with Skanderborg was not to his advantage 
2 CONC because of the poor condition of the crown estate there. Alongside this, entries had been made in his accounts, but he still had a claim on the crown of over 6000 Rdl. In Nov. He now applied for an improvement in his fief conditio
2 CONC ns in return for the surrender of this claim. It seems that an arrangement was then made that he should give up his fief, but that in return for this and the 6,000 rdl. he should be given an annual pension of 6,000 rdl. In April 1
2 CONC 661 he also surrendered the fief. But shortly afterwards, in June, the agreements were changed to the effect that he was granted crown property in Kalø for 20,000 rdl., and his pension was reduced to 1,000 rdl. However, he still s
2 CONC eems to have had other claims on the crown, and the 1,000 rdl. were not paid to him for several years. He had retained his position as a member of the Royal Council after the change of government and had become a member of the Sup
2 CONC reme Court, but he had not obtained a seat in the other colleges. However, his private creditors came after him. He then decided to go bankrupt in 1662. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Despite an objection from the Attorney General Peder Scavenius on the occasion of the previously mentioned accounting entries, things came to order; but he was left without funds, and was also threatened with a series of lawsuits 
2 CONC from his creditors. Again and again he applied to the government to have his pension and his claim paid and even a royal barn assigned to him for residence; but it quickly became clear that he had no friends. It cannot be denied t
2 CONC hat political motives had come into play here. The king's non-Danish advisors had hardly forgotten his attacks on them, and he was even among those who were accused at court, according to the expression in a short piece about him,
2 CONC  of "inciting the restitution of the corrupt nature of previous times." He was also a man whose entire financial situation gave rise to many exposures, and it only worsened his position that in the winter of 1664, perhaps for comp
2 CONC letely innocent reasons, he paid the Duke of Gottorp a visit. When he sought a scholarship from the King for one of his sons in the summer, he received the answer that he could approach the Duke about it. He felt his position in d
2 CONC espair, especially since a Supreme Court session was at the door with numerous processes. Then it hit him on the head that he was told by his brother-in-law, the Minister of Finance, Mogens Friis, that the King demanded his resign
2 CONC ation as a member of the Supreme Court because of his position as a bankrupt, and that he should therefore seek this. The result was that he addressed the Swedish Resident in Copenhagen and informed him that he wanted to flee to S
2 CONC weden and seek the protection of Charles XI, with the humiliating addition that he hoped for the king's generosity despite the fact that in serving his king he had sinned against the Swedish nation. He quickly made his decision se
2 CONC rious, sent his wife and daughters ahead of him and took a boat to Helsingborg the next day, in the last week of July . He never saw Denmark again. By a letter to the councilors he asked for leave. But his fate was only sad. The D
2 CONC anish government did indeed give up a firm decision to summon him to the Supreme Court, but the defense papers he prepared made no impression, and while he wandered around Sweden, poor and abandoned and begging for help from Swedi
2 CONC sh ministers in the most groveling tone, the Swedish government did nothing for him. Immediately before the death of Frederick III, he asked the king for permission to return home; he received the answer: I do not know that man. T
2 CONC hen he sought  formally to become a Swedish subject with the words that he "had no higher ambition than to live and die as good a Swedish patriot as I have now lived a Danish patriot for 66 years". No answer came. He turned again 
2 CONC to Denmark, but received a message from Christian V that he had not asked him to leave and would not ask him to come again. He sought Swedish service again; again in vain. Then he died in Helsingborg in 1675.
2 CONT
